WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:29.980
Peace be upon you and the mercy of God and His blessings. Welcome to the second video. In the previous video, we realized that the health educator is the first line of defense. In this video, we want to think about how we can turn complex, entangled health and climate numbers and data into tangible daily habits that people truly apply. Today, we are before an excellent opportunity to understand this secret because we will live together through the journey of Sarah. Sarah is a community health educator:

00:30.000 --> 00:44.140
She goes down to the street in the heart of neighborhoods full of movement and life, so that we can explore how to move beyond merely lining up words and information to a stage where we build true bridges engineering community behavior.

00:44.140 --> 00:58.920
Why does knowledge alone not always change behavior? This is the question that frustrates anyone working in awareness. Imagine with me this scene: Sarah is standing in a very crowded neighborhood, seeing exhausted workers whose sweat is dripping,

00:58.920 --> 01:10.920
and ordinary mothers running under this scorching sun. All these people know that severe heat can harm and kill a person, and that water is life,

01:10.920 --> 01:19.640
exactly like we all know that smoking is harmful. Yet despite that, you rarely find this knowledge translated into real change in people's actions.

01:19.640 --> 01:24.200
Knowledge alone, quite simply, does not possess enough power to move people.

01:24.200 --> 01:28.380
The decisive point here that Sarah discovered while on the ground:

01:28.920 --> 01:35.800
The problem was never that we lacked printing extra colored papers and posters, or that our medical information was inaccurate;

01:35.800 --> 01:44.640
the real problem is that there is no genuine link to people's daily lives and living. When we throw information at people as dry and stiff facts,

01:44.640 --> 01:50.080
we always fail to close this gap. What we need is not merely talk;

01:50.080 --> 01:56.040
what we need is to build a solid bridge connecting science with people's daily reality and their minds.

01:56.040 --> 01:58.800
This takes us straight to the first principle in direct communication:

01:58.920 --> 02:01.300
Evidence-based tailoring of the message.

02:01.300 --> 02:04.980
A single message can never succeed and fit everyone's measurements.

02:04.980 --> 02:08.300
Sarah intelligently grasped that flexibility is the key:

02:08.300 --> 02:13.780
It does not work to deal with all people in the exact same manner and expect the same outcome.

02:13.780 --> 02:20.060
For example, when she entered an elementary school, she did not sit giving them a boring lecture about dehydration;

02:20.060 --> 02:23.640
no, she told them a story about the Water Superhero,

02:23.640 --> 02:26.580
who fights the dehydration monster with his secret weapon:

02:26.580 --> 02:27.820
the water bottle.

02:27.820 --> 02:28.780
The result?

02:28.920 --> 02:31.080
The children all ran to drink water.

02:31.080 --> 02:36.040
Yet when she went right after that to a construction site working under the scorching sun,

02:36.040 --> 02:38.060
she eliminated the superhero story completely,

02:38.060 --> 02:39.980
and spoke with the laborers in another language:

02:39.980 --> 02:44.080
She told them: "Your health is your capital and the strength of your families."

02:44.080 --> 02:48.740
She linked heat stress to their ability to work and feed their households.

02:48.740 --> 02:52.000
The psychological explanation of what Sarah did is wonderful:

02:52.000 --> 02:54.580
The human mind possesses natural filters;

02:54.580 --> 02:58.880
it is programmed automatically to reject and discard any information it feels is alien to it,

02:58.880 --> 03:01.300
or not touching the pain of its reality. Meaning:

03:01.300 --> 03:05.160
If the listener does not feel that we speak their language and feel their pain,

03:05.160 --> 03:07.340
they will shut the door of reception immediately.

03:07.340 --> 03:10.040
We must reach hearts before minds,

03:10.040 --> 03:14.880
and this begins by knowing who our audience is and tailoring the message to their exact size.

03:15.680 --> 03:19.000
The second station in our journey reveals the second principle:

03:19.000 --> 03:19.760
Simplifying science.

03:19.760 --> 03:24.080
Often we think that to appear knowledgeable and professional,

03:24.080 --> 03:26.880
we must use complex scientific terms.

03:26.880 --> 03:32.280
Yet look at what happened when Sarah's colleague tried to explain the hazards of air pollution to people,

03:32.280 --> 03:36.820
and began speaking about fine particulate matter and its effect on alveoli:

03:36.820 --> 03:41.100
The result: People tuned out, grew bored, and became confused.

03:41.100 --> 03:43.560
Complexity is the primary enemy of change.

03:43.560 --> 03:46.940
Dry academic terminology creates cognitive load,

03:46.940 --> 03:48.940
making the person in front of you disconnect from you completely.

03:48.940 --> 03:50.060
To communicate properly,

03:50.060 --> 03:53.700
human translation of terminology is an inevitable necessity, not a luxury.

03:53.700 --> 03:55.240
Keep this as a rule in your mind:

03:55.240 --> 03:59.700
If you cannot explain your idea to a ten-year-old child so they understand it,

03:59.700 --> 04:01.760
then you have not simplified it well yet.

04:01.760 --> 04:05.900
And so Sarah intervened calmly to rescue the situation,

04:05.900 --> 04:08.900
and gave a very clever visual analogy:

04:08.900 --> 04:13.700
She told them: "Imagine that your lungs are like a clean white sponge,

04:13.700 --> 04:16.880
and this polluted air is as if you are pouring black motor oil over it."

04:16.880 --> 04:19.280
As soon as she spoke these two simple words,

04:19.280 --> 04:21.980
the complete silence turned into genuine interaction,

04:21.980 --> 04:23.740
and parents began asking:

04:23.740 --> 04:25.900
"Well, how can we protect this sponge?"

04:25.900 --> 04:28.280
Transforming unseen things like particulates

04:28.280 --> 04:30.920
into familiar images in our minds like a sponge and oil

04:30.920 --> 04:31.920
creates engagement.

04:31.920 --> 04:34.400
This takes us to the third principle:

04:34.400 --> 04:36.240
Establishing psychological proximity:

04:36.240 --> 04:39.500
How to make major global issues like climate change

04:39.500 --> 04:42.360
become personal matters concerning people in the street and at home.

04:42.360 --> 04:45.020
Here comes the role of psychological proximity:

04:45.020 --> 04:48.160
We as humans are moved by personal connection and emotion;

04:48.160 --> 04:53.640
issues we feel are merely distant from us geographically and temporally

04:53.640 --> 04:56.280
will not motivate us to act or do anything.

04:56.280 --> 04:59.540
The goal here is to transform distant general anxiety

04:59.540 --> 05:01.500
into direct daily concern

05:01.500 --> 05:03.780
that pushes us to search for solutions.

05:03.780 --> 05:06.220
To clarify the difference in practical application:

05:06.220 --> 05:09.840
Imagine if we continued talking about ice melting in the North Pole:

05:09.840 --> 05:13.380
People would feel it is a story on television with which they have no involvement.

05:13.380 --> 05:15.740
Yet Sarah shifted the angle completely:

05:15.740 --> 05:18.020
While speaking with mothers in the neighborhood,

05:18.160 --> 05:21.980
she asked them: "Did you not notice that asthma and choking episodes among your children

05:21.980 --> 05:23.780
increased with waves of dust and heat?

05:23.780 --> 05:26.860
Did you not notice how vegetables became expensive

05:26.860 --> 05:29.720
because crops were hit by water scarcity and drought?"

05:29.720 --> 05:33.100
Suddenly, climate was no longer a guest on television screens;

05:33.100 --> 05:37.000
it became a direct threat to their children's health and their household expenses.

05:37.000 --> 05:39.120
Finally, we arrive at the fourth principle:

05:39.120 --> 05:42.960
Formulating clear calls to action:

05:42.960 --> 05:44.840
The biggest mistake we can make in any communication

05:44.840 --> 05:47.700
is leaving people feeling helpless or fearful

05:47.700 --> 05:49.380
after explaining the problem to them,

05:49.380 --> 05:51.260
without giving them a clear path to a solution.

05:51.260 --> 05:53.340
That is why at the end of her day, Sarah

05:53.340 --> 05:56.440
did not tell people grand, vague slogans

05:56.440 --> 05:58.160
like "Save the planet" and such;

05:58.160 --> 06:00.100
talk like that causes paralysis in thinking.

06:00.100 --> 06:03.680
Instead, she gave them specific, manageable steps:

06:03.680 --> 06:06.020
She asked each one to do a simple thing tomorrow:

06:06.020 --> 06:07.800
Drink an extra liter of water,

06:07.800 --> 06:10.760
place a bowl of water in the shade for birds,

06:10.760 --> 06:13.100
or plant a small seedling in front of their home.

06:13.100 --> 06:16.440
The psychological impact of these small steps is truly incredible:

06:16.440 --> 06:18.840
Knowledge with no direct application

06:18.840 --> 06:21.120
leads us to what is called analysis paralysis:

06:21.120 --> 06:23.520
We sit thinking a lot and do nothing.

06:23.520 --> 06:26.100
Yet these small tasks break the intimidation of change,

06:26.100 --> 06:28.080
giving a person a sense of achievement,

06:28.080 --> 06:30.040
and that they are capable of protecting their neighborhood and family.

06:30.040 --> 06:32.900
When we begin applying small, manageable tasks,

06:32.900 --> 06:35.420
we transform from passive listeners

06:35.420 --> 06:36.980
into an active part of the solution.

06:36.980 --> 06:39.880
This is what builds genuine commitment continuing forward.

06:39.880 --> 06:42.620
Here we stand before a completely new role:

06:42.620 --> 06:45.140
We are behavioral engineers and change makers.

06:45.140 --> 06:47.860
Let us bring all these principles together in summary:

06:47.860 --> 06:51.260
Sarah's journey is summarized in four core flashes:

06:51.260 --> 06:55.260
Tailor the message to match the needs and language of the people we speak with;

06:55.260 --> 06:57.700
simplify complex scientific concepts

06:57.700 --> 06:59.060
so they are easily digested;

06:59.060 --> 07:02.240
create psychological proximity by linking major issues

07:02.240 --> 07:03.620
to their daily details;

07:03.620 --> 07:07.220
and crown all this with small, clear steps and requests

07:07.220 --> 07:09.240
that prevent helplessness and encourage achievement.

07:09.240 --> 07:11.840
We are not merely people who convey information;

07:11.840 --> 07:14.720
we translate science so it touches the ground of reality,

07:14.720 --> 07:18.540
and engineer behavior because we understand the psychology of the person facing us well.

07:18.540 --> 07:22.420
True impact is measured by actual change in people's daily lives,

07:22.420 --> 07:26.000
not by stacking facts or dumping a handful of information and leaving.

07:26.000 --> 07:29.540
Finally, this explanation leaves us before a truly inspiring challenge:

07:29.540 --> 07:34.240
Next time we want to speak with our families, colleagues at work, or neighborhood residents:

07:34.240 --> 07:36.640
How will we take this dry, complex information

07:36.640 --> 07:41.120
and turn it into a story filled with soul that can change a simple daily habit?

07:41.120 --> 07:45.320
The whole matter begins with understanding the human being before understanding the science.

07:45.320 --> 07:49.160
In the next video, we will transition together to the field step:

07:49.160 --> 07:54.780
How to identify the groups most vulnerable and affected by climate variations in our neighborhood,

07:54.780 --> 07:57.340
and tailor the right awareness programs for them.

07:57.340 --> 07:59.340
Thank you for being with us in this explanation,

07:59.340 --> 08:02.520
and we always look forward to discovering more things with you.
